  "items": [
    {
      "id": "p-chart-patterns",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "skills": [
        "cnn-train"
      ],
      "prose": "A CNN sorts 300 held-out chart images into head-and-shoulders, double top and no pattern, 100 of each. Its confusion rows (true class, then read as head-and-shoulders, double top, none) are $(88, 8, 4)$, $(6, 90, 4)$ and $(2, 3, 95)$.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "question": "What is its held-out accuracy? Give a decimal to 2 places.",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 0.91,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0.005
            },
            "placeholder": "a decimal",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "one-class",
                "value": 0.88,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "errors",
                "value": 0.09,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$(88 + 90 + 95)/300 = 273/300 = 0.91$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Add the diagonal and divide by 300.",
            "byKey": {
              "one-class": "That is one row. Use all three.",
              "errors": "That is the error rate."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "The diagonal holds 273 of 300: 0.91."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "Which class does it read worst, and what is its per-class accuracy? Give that accuracy as a decimal to 2 places.",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 0.88,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0.005
            },
            "placeholder": "a decimal",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "headline",
                "value": 0.91,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "column",
                "value": 0.9167,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "Head-and-shoulders: $88/100 = 0.88$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Divide each diagonal entry by its row of 100.",
            "byKey": {
              "headline": "That is the accuracy of part 1, over all classes.",
              "column": "That divides by the column. Per-class accuracy divides by the row."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "The rows give 0.88, 0.90 and 0.95; head-and-shoulders is worst."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "Using the worst class from part 2, what would the held-out accuracy be if every chart of that class were read right and nothing else changed? Give a decimal to 2 places.",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 0.95,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0.005
            },
            "placeholder": "a decimal",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "class",
                "value": 1.0,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "part1",
                "value": 0.91,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$(273 + 12)/300 = 285/300 = 0.95$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Add the 12 fixed charts to the 273 right ones.",
            "byKey": {
              "class": "That is the class alone. The headline counts all 300.",
              "part1": "That is the accuracy before the fix."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "Fixing the 12 misread head-and-shoulders charts gives 285 of 300, which is 0.95."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "Of the charts it reads as double top, what share really are double tops? Give a decimal to 4 places.",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 0.8911,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 5e-05
            },
            "placeholder": "a decimal",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "row",
                "value": 0.9,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.0001
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "all",
                "value": 0.3,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.0001
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$90/(8 + 90 + 3) = 90/101 = 0.8911$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Read the double-top column.",
            "byKey": {
              "row": "That is the row: the share of true double tops it finds.",
              "all": "That divides by all 300."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "The column holds 101 charts read as double top, 90 of them real: 0.8911, below the class’s per-class accuracy of 0.90."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-wider-net",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "skills": [
        "cnn-train"
      ],
      "prose": "A research net reads 8 by 8 inputs through 32 filters of 3 by 3 (padding 1). It halves each side by pooling in 2 by 2 windows and ends in 10 scores from one dense layer.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "question": "How many parameters does it have?",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 5450,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            },
            "placeholder": "a whole number",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "no-pool",
                "value": 20810,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "conv-only",
                "value": 320,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$32 \\times 10 = 320$, $512 \\times 10 + 10 = 5{,}130$: 5,450.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Count the convolution and the dense layer.",
            "byKey": {
              "no-pool": "That reads the 2,048 values before pooling.",
              "conv-only": "Add the dense layer on the 512 pooled values."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "The convolution has $32 \\times (9 + 1) = 320$; pooling leaves $32 \\times 4 \\times 4 = 512$ values, so the dense layer has 5,130 and the net 5,450."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "A residual block of two 3 by 3 convolutions, 32 channels to 32, goes after the first convolution. How many parameters does the block add?",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 18496,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            },
            "placeholder": "a whole number",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "one",
                "value": 9248,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "no-bias",
                "value": 18432,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$2 \\times 32 \\times (32 \\times 9 + 1) = 18{,}496$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Two convolutions, each reading 32 channels.",
            "byKey": {
              "one": "That is one convolution.",
              "no-bias": "Each filter has a bias."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "Each convolution has $32 \\times 289 = 9{,}248$, so the block adds 18,496."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "Using parts 1 and 2, how many parameters does the residual net have in all?",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 23946,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            },
            "placeholder": "a whole number",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "block",
                "value": 18496,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "base",
                "value": 5450,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$5{,}450 + 18{,}496 = 23{,}946$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Add the block to the base net.",
            "byKey": {
              "block": "Add the base net of part 1.",
              "base": "Add the block of part 2."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "The block more than quadruples the net, to 23,946."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "At one input the slope of the block’s $F$ is $-0.3$. What is the slope of the block’s output $y = x + F(x)$ there? Give a decimal to 1 place.",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 0.7,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0.05
            },
            "placeholder": "a decimal",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "no-skip",
                "value": -0.3,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "sign",
                "value": 1.3,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.001
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$1 + (-0.3) = 0.7$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. The identity path adds 1.",
            "byKey": {
              "no-skip": "That is $F$ alone. Add the identity path.",
              "sign": "The slope of $F$ is negative."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "However many parameters the block of part 2 adds, its identity path keeps a 1 in the gradient: $1 - 0.3 = 0.7$."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-shift-gap",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "skills": [
        "cnn-train"
      ],
      "prose": "On the four one-pixel shifts of a held-out set, a CNN averages 0.64 and a same-size MLP 0.47. The exit asks for a gap of at least 0.10.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "question": "What is the gap between the two nets? Give a decimal to 2 places.",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 0.17,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0.005
            },
            "placeholder": "a decimal",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "ratio",
                "value": 1.36,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.005
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$0.64 - 0.47 = 0.17$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Subtract the MLP from the CNN.",
            "byKey": {
              "ratio": "That is the ratio. The rule uses the difference."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "The gap is 0.17."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "Does the gap from part 1 meet the clear-margin rule?",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "choice",
            "layout": "stack",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "Yes: 0.17 is at least 0.10",
                "correct": true
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "No: the CNN is below 0.98",
                "why": "The 0.98 bar is for unshifted digits; this part asks about the margin."
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "No: both nets lose accuracy",
                "why": "Losing accuracy is expected; the rule compares the losses."
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "It depends on the clean accuracies",
                "why": "The rule uses the shifted means alone."
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "A gap of 0.17 clears 0.10.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Compare the gap with 0.10."
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "The margin compares mean shifted accuracies: 0.17 is above 0.10."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "Both nets are retrained on the training set plus four shifted copies, 1,005 images each. In batches of 32, how many Adam steps is one epoch?",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 158,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            },
            "placeholder": "a whole number",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "floor",
                "value": 157,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "plain",
                "value": 32,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$\\lceil 5{,}025/32 \\rceil = 158$.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Count the augmented images first.",
            "byKey": {
              "floor": "That drops the last short batch of 1 image.",
              "plain": "That is the unaugmented set. Five copies make 5,025 images."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "Five copies of 1,005 make 5,025 images; 157 full batches hold 5,024, so a 158th batch takes the last image."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "After augmenting, the CNN averages 0.96 and the MLP 0.93 on the shifts. Unlike the 0.17 of part 1, what is the gap now? Give a decimal to 2 places.",
          "widget": {
            "kind": "numeric",
            "answer": 0.03,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0.005
            },
            "placeholder": "a decimal",
            "wrong": [
              {
                "id": "old",
                "value": 0.17,
                "tol": {
                  "abs": 0.005
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "feedback": {
            "correct": "$0.96 - 0.93 = 0.03$: below the rule’s 0.10.",
            "generic": "Not quite. Subtract the new means.",
            "byKey": {
              "old": "That is the gap before augmenting."
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "text": "Shifted copies teach the MLP most of what convolution builds in: the gap falls from 0.17 to 0.03."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
